    "q-171821": {
      "dom_id": "step-q171821",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14133186/assets/89c36c6195b98d333a7fedaf2bd2fe72b8c7b334aa92b85a5cc0ba1fc1145897.png)\n\nSince the measure of $∠BAD$ in the diagram above is ${85}^{∘},$ the angle $x$ must be equal to{{field-1}}${}^{∘}.$",
      "worked_solution": "Here, the letter $x$ represents the measure of the unknown angle $∠CAD.$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The angles $∠BAC$ and $∠CAD$ are adjacent because they share the side $\\overset{\\to }{AC}.$\n\n- So, the measure of the *large* angle $(∠BAD)$ must be the *sum* of the measures of the two *smaller* angles $(∠BAC$ and $∠CAD).$\n\n- This means that the measure of $∠CAD$ must be the *difference* between the large angle $(∠BAD)$ and the smaller known angle $(∠BAC).$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nx={85}^{∘}-{40}^{∘}={45}^{∘}.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
